Dominic Raab: The government cannot apologize for its decision to isolate Spain.
Dominic Raab justified the government's sudden decision to reintroduce quarantine measures for Spain.
The ministers announced on Saturday that those who fail to return to Spain and its islands before midnight will be required to self-isolate for 14 days.
Spain has been on the list of “travel corridors” that can safely travel as set out at Number 10 Downing Street, but recent increases in coronavirus cases have raised public concerns about a second wave of the pandemic.
On Sunday, the Foreign Minister justified the sudden reinstatement of travel restrictions by saying it was a "real-time response" to the spike in reported infections in the region.
He stated that he had assessed the situation the previous afternoon, and "we made a quick decision."
He added, “We do not apologize for this.”
“We must be able to take swift and decisive action, especially in cases involving our homeland or Spain or specific countries.”
Otherwise, Britain would face another infection risk, triggering a second wave of the pandemic, and have to impose another lockdown.
The Minister of Transport faces mandatory quarantine just like vacationers heading to Spain.
Following two consecutive days of reporting more than 900 new coronavirus cases per day in Spain, the government made this decision.
The Foreign Minister acknowledged that many tourists were unable to cancel or change their travel plans in time.
He said, “I know that for those in Spain or considering going to Spain, this decision is extremely unfeeling, but we must have the capacity to take swift and decisive action to protect Britain.”
"We have made notable progress in reducing the case numbers and preventing a resurgence of the epidemic."
Shadow Health Minister Jonathan Ashworth condemned the government’s approach as “chaotic.”
He said the sudden decision at Downing Street left holidaymakers "confused and disappointed."
This Labour Party member told Sophie Ridge, "I understand why they made this decision, but their decision-making over the past 24 hours has been chaotic."
Holidaymakers in Spain right now are confused and disappointed, and so are those planning to holiday in Spain or in other islands such as Tenerife, who are unsure whether their employers will accept a two-week quarantine.
“The government is just saying ‘we hope employers will cooperate.’”
“There was no logic to their decision in the past 24 hours.”
The CDC notes that Salmonella is a bacterial foodborne illness that causes 1.35 million infections annually, leads to hospitalization of 26,500 people, and results in 420 deaths.
Common symptoms after infection with Salmonella include diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps, which may begin to appear six hours to six days after exposure to the bacteria.
Symptoms usually last for four to seven days, and most people recover without treatment, but some cases require hospitalization.
California Dog Park Couple Attacked, Both Because They Were Not Masked During Lunch (Video) – RT News, US News
First the mask shame, then the punches and kicks.
A couple in California were confronted with this when they had lunch with their dog at a dog park. A woman sprayed pepper spray at them because they were not wearing masks while eating.
Ash O'Brien and her husband Jarett Kelley say they were eating lunch at the Dusty Rhodes Dog Park in San Diego with their three-month-old Beagle on Thursday.
As the couple took off their masks to eat, an "old lady who had suddenly appeared" came over and called them "idiots" because they were not wearing masks, even though they said "how can we eat with masks on."
O’Brien forced the woman out of the park and stopped her harassment at which point she sprayed pepper spray at them.
The video taken by bystanders begins with Kelley fighting for the spray can with a woman, and he ends up spraying himself in the face.
After the attackers left with their dogs, one could hear O’Brien crying in the background.
O'Brien uploaded this video to Facebook and told ABC 10 that he hopes the woman "goes to prison."
California requires masks to be worn statewide, even in places with fresh air.
People can only take off their masks during designated times, such as when eating.
However, the state’s COVID-19 guidelines do not mention that bystanders can use non-lethal weapons to enforce this requirement.
Even in this “mask shaming” era, it is a crime punishable by a $1,000 fine or less than three years in state prison.
The urchin from Chandni Chowk who was flipped over for refusing a bribe to a driver later received a set of apartments and free education worth 100 rupees in a bribe.
It was reported that Thursday, Paras Raykar's car, filled with eggs, was overturned by Indore municipal officials.
The story of a 13-year-old boy who failed to sell eggs and had his car overturned by municipal officials spread on social media, and two days later, the boy from Indore received support from all over the country to help them recover their losses and restart their business.
The boy's family also said that politicians including Nationalist Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal had reached out to them and offered help as well.
The boy's family also received an apartment as a gift and a promise to cover their children's educational expenses.
It was reported that on Thursday, Paras Raykar's cart selling eggs was overturned by Indore municipal officials, as the city implemented a staggered opening from left to right amid the pandemic.
In a video, a boy says that officials warned him that they would flip his car if he did not pay a bribe of 100 rupees that morning. The video quickly spread on social media.
The boy says that when he refused to hand over the 100 rupees, those officials overturned his car and smashed all the eggs.
The boy also mentions in the video that due to the impact of the epidemic, the daily sales of eggs have declined, and this loss has been especially hard for him.
Since Thursday, the family of the boy has received support from all over the country, with many offering cash or kind assistance.
The locals also extended a helping hand to this family.
Former Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh funded them and helped the children enroll in a college; People's Party MP Ramesh Mendola also assured that a flat would be provided to the boy's family under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
The Indore Press Club also provided food and money for the boy.
The office of Congress Party leader Rahul Gandhi and Delhi Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal also stepped in.
Paras' grandfather said, "We have received support from many people across the country."
(RMT Member) Ramesh Mendola assures us that he will provide us with an apartment according to the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana housing scheme.
He provided a bicycle and a grant of 2,500 rupees.
Digvijaya Singh contributed 10,000 rupees and also pledged to look after the education of the two children.
Rahul Gandhi's staff also called us for help.
A female staff member of Chief Minister Arvind Kejriwal in Delhi also called us and helped us.
Jyothiraditya Scindia also gave his support.”
The Home Secretary Priti Patel has asked Twitter and Instagram to explain the inappropriate content posted by Wiley.
Due to Twitter and Instagram’s failure to promptly remove inappropriate content published by Wiley, Priti Patel requested explanations from both platforms.
The police are currently investigating a series of anti-Semitic remarks made by this Grime artist on social media.
The Minister of Home Affairs tweeted on Sunday: "Wiley's anti-Semitic content is deeply offensive."
Twitter and Instagram did not promptly delete these posts, and I have asked them for a full explanation.
Social media companies must act faster to remove these shocking hate speech from their platforms.”
He criticized Twitter for ignoring antisemitism because Wiley's tweets remained visible 12 hours after they were posted.
Subsequently, the website deleted multiple tweets and banned the account of the 41-year-old singer for seven days.
Twitter previously stated that it had temporarily suspended Wiley’s account “due to violations of our hate speech policy,” while Facebook said that “hate speech is not allowed on Instagram.”
On Saturday, the NYPD said, "The NYPD is extremely concerned about all reports of anti-Semitism. We are currently evaluating the relevant materials."
Meanwhile, Wiley’s agency A-List has also severed all relationships with this star.
John Woolf, the former agent of the singer, wrote on Twitter: "Given the anti-Semitic content Wiley published today, we @A_ListMGMT have severed all ties with him. There is no place for anti-Semitism in this society."
Earlier, he had indicated that he neither supported nor condoned Wiley’s comments but would engage with him privately to “educate” him.
However, an Instagram video appears to show Wiley severing ties with his management company.
Other lawmakers criticized Twitter for failing to remove the posts promptly.
Labour MP Jess Phillips said on Saturday: "I just saw what Wiley published. Why is @Twitter allowing such blatant antisemitism and hate content to remain? It involves dangerous views: Jews have things you don't have, they control everything, they think they're superior, and so on. Such content is dangerous and should be deleted."
Neil Coyle, the member, added: "His PR company seems to have reacted faster than @Twitter, and I’d like to reiterate that legislation (including the Online Harms Bill) must be put in place to ensure that social media platforms are not used to spread hatred."
Government anti-Semitic independent adviser John Mann called the tweets "a precise reflection of why legal changes are needed to hold people accountable."
The campaign to combat anti-Semitism also called for police to investigate Wiley’s content and called for the closure of his social media accounts “to prevent another spewing of anti-Semitic venom.”
Wiley, whose real name is Richard Cowie, is known as the "Grime Music Father" and was awarded an MBE in 2018 for his contributions to music.
Kudlow said that the next coronavirus aid package would include more stimulus checks and extend eviction moratoria.
White House economic adviser Larry Kudlow promised on Sunday that the federal government would extend the moratorium on evictions and distribute another round of stimulus checks according to the next coronavirus relief plan.
The week before Kudlow made his pledge, the White House’s proposal to reduce payroll taxes had been rejected by Congress.
The residents of Massart Mallawi village refused to comment on the incident and only offered condolences to the families of the children.
National Women and Children’s Committee advisor Khalid Darwish said in October that approximately 93% of Egyptian children between the ages of 1 and 14 have suffered from violence.
He also noted that prosecution is one of the most important means to eliminate child abuse, and stressed the need for enhanced cooperation between the Committee and the public prosecutors in this regard.
A research conducted by the National Committee on Women and Children found that children can also be subjected to violence even in places where they should be relatively safe, such as their homes, schools, or communities, with perpetrators including those who are supposed to care for them—such as parents or teachers.
AP criticizes White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany's "political" briefing, but journalists continue to ask political questions – RT TV America News
AP criticizes White House Press Secretary Kayleigh McEnany for making the press conference more political than newsworthy, worrying the media, but apparently forgetting that it was the journalists who chose those questions.
The largest US news agency, the Associated Press, published an article on Saturday stating that the White House press conference "may have broken tradition" because McEnany turned the press conference into a "short and flashy campaign to promote and attack the media for the president's re-election," while there is "a relative lack of information about what the government has done."
The last review is understandable as very little new information was disclosed at the press conference.
What the Associated Press does not mention is that virtually no one asked for any new information at the press conference.
Journalists often use White House press officers to draw attention to themselves, and Jim Acosta of CNN took this approach to the extreme.
They transcend the common adversarial relationship between the media and government, repeatedly asking loaded questions to doubt President Donald Trump.
In some instances, they choose to become the news themselves, such as when CBS reporter Weijia Jiang made an unambiguous implication in response to Trump’s retort of “ask China”: “Sir, why did you address this to me specifically?”
The launch on July 6 is a good example of how it is no longer a source of reliable information for American citizens.
On that morning, Trump tweeted that NASCAR ratings had declined because of false reporting about so-called hate crimes against driver Bubba Wallace and because he decided to ban displaying the Confederate flag during events.
Just as expected, reporters one after another asked McEnany questions related to this tweet, trying to get her to publicly admit that Trump supports the Confederate flag.
McEnany did not allow the reporters to get away with it, as that would have damaged Trump’s interests, but she repeatedly responded that the president has no position on the Confederate flag, and his tweets center on mischaracterizing NASCAR fans as racists.
Associated Press reported that she sounded “outraged” during the press conference.
The Associated Press story echoes a recent column in The Washington Post by Jonathan Karl, an ABC News reporter and president of the White House Correspondents’ Association.
Karl says, "Denying reality and using the White House rostrum for purely political purposes goes against public trust."
McEnany took over as press secretary in April and hosted her first press conference on May 1st, and her quick-wittedness and combative attitude made some Trump supporters see her as a hero.
Before she was appointed, the position of press officer had been vacant for more than a year.
Like other aspects of Washington's political life, the relationship between Trump-era White House press secretary and the media underwent significant changes.
This change came from both sides, having already occurred before McEnany's arrival.
At the White House Correspondents' Dinner in April 2018, comedian Michelle Wolf subjected former Press Secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders to vicious questioning, even MSNBC host Mika Brzezinski called it "shocking."
AP White House correspondent Jill Colvin asked McEnany at her first press conference, "Can you promise never to lie to us from that podium?"
Fox News political analyst Brit Hume, a seasoned journalist with over 50 years of experience, who served as ABC’s chief White House correspondent from 1989 to 1996, says that today’s journalists exhibit “aggressive behavior” at press conferences because they look down on Trump.
He told Tucker Carlson Tonight on Fox News this week that it’s because reporters have been focused on countering Trump, and ignoring the collection of policy news.
"This is not just a game of cat and mouse," Hume said in discussing the media's intended purpose.
Our goal is not just to bring down government officials but to obtain information.
California protesters set fire to a courthouse
Oakland (California) – Oakland police said that late Saturday night, peaceful protests in California turned into violent incidents. Protesters set fire to a courthouse, smashed police stations, and attacked several officers.
The Auckland authorities stated on Twitter that protesters smashed windows, sprayed graffiti, and threw fireworks and lasers at police personnel.
Some called for peace on Twitter, with tweets requesting organizers to "help us provide safe spaces and locations for the protesters."
Protests began on Saturday night, with organizations such as "Mothers Wall" (analogous to an organization in Portland, Oregon) participating in the demonstrations. At the same time, protesters confronted police deployed to the federal courthouse for security duties.
Since the May 25 George Floyd incident in Minneapolis, President Donald Trump has sent federal police to suppress demonstrations every night.
Initially, the protesters held peaceful demonstrations in Auckland, but later turned into violent clashes in the evening.
At around 11:30 PM, the police declared the protest an "illegal assembly" and demanded that the demonstrators disperse.
According to news reports, a fire broke out at the Alameda County Superior Court, but the fire was brought under control in a short time.
Police said that protesters had at one point “broken glass and made racially motivated insults to residents.”
The photos posted by relevant departments show that the glass of the police station building has been broken, and there are graffiti all over the wall.
UN senior officials canceled their visit to Pakistan due to flight reasons.
Islamabad (AP) – The incoming president of the United Nations General Assembly announced on Sunday that his visit to Pakistan had been delayed “due to technical problems with the flights.”
Last month, Turkish diplomat Volkan Bozkir successfully became the President of the 75th session of the United Nations General Assembly, which was originally scheduled to arrive in Islamabad on Sunday.
However, he tweeted that his plan to visit the Pakistani capital had been delayed.
Pakistan's Foreign Minister Shah Mahmood Qureshi tweeted in response to Bozkir, saying "We look forward to your swift visit to Pakistan for a constructive and productive visit."
Just one day before, Qureshi had excitedly announced his visit, hoping to discuss issues such as Kashmir with Bozkir, which is located between Pakistan and its neighboring country India, and both claim sovereignty over the entire region of Kashmir.
Since achieving independence from British rule in 1947, there have been three wars between Pakistan and India, both of which involved the disputed region.
Frequent clashes between the two sides in Kashmir result in military and civilian casualties.
Pakistan hopes that the UN will adopt a resolution allowing people living in the Himalayan region to decide whether to merge with India or Pakistan or become independent, thus pushing for a solution to the Kashmir issue.
Manchester United secured a place in the Champions League, and Paul Pogba hinted at staying with the team.
Paul Pogba, Manchester United star, hinted that he would continue to stay with the club this summer after the Red Devils secured a place in the quarterfinals.
Claiming Leicester City puts Manchester United in third place, ahead of Chelsea under Frank Lampard’s management.
Before 12 games, Leicester City was still leading Manchester United by 14 points, however the Red Devils made a strong push to reach the quarter-finals.
Pogba has twice requested to leave the team in the past two years, which has led fans to predict that he will depart this summer.
However, the pandemic means that few teams will be spending heavily this summer, and there is no buyer who can afford both the transfer fee and the wages needed to lure Pogba away from Old Trafford.
Making such a statement has been helpful for Pogba in the past few weeks, as his performance and body language have improved.
After winning the match 2-0, the French player posted on Instagram suggesting that he would stay with the team.
While Pogba may simply be celebrating Juventus’ return to the Champions League, many fans interpret him as strongly hinting that he will continue with the top European players at the club.
Pogba's current contract has one year remaining, but the club can extend it for another year, meaning he would actually be under contract for two more years.
Manchester United is eager to extend Pogba's contract and hopes to clarify his situation before the upcoming season.
For Pogba, he feels comfortable in the team, and Bruno Fernandes' arrival gives him an extra boost.
This Portuguese midfielder became the heart of Old Trafford, allowing Pogba to advance deeper into the midfield and improve his mood.
Aubameyang helps Arsenal send Wolverhampton Wanderers into English Premier League relegation.
Some of Watford’s players knelt to the ground, while others turned their heads away and left the field without looking back.
The whistle for Watford’s return to英超 in its fifth year has barely blown, but no one can say that Watford did not succumb without a fight as it desperately chased Arsenal.
In fact, this was Watford’s most spirited and relentless performance in five years, coming back from a three-point deficit but ultimately losing to Arsenal.
Just 16 minutes before the end of the match, Danny Welbeck shot with a heel strike, and goalkeeper Emiliano Martínez saved the ball. Had he scored, Welbeck would have earned three points for Watford, equaling Arsenal's score and securing their spot in the Premier League.
But in the end, Watford failed to achieve this, and their caretaker manager Hayden Mullins stated that if their previous form had been as good as it was in this match, he would not have had to exert himself so much.
Throughout the season, Watford struggled, and owner Pozzo's management of the team was also chaotic.
A failed campaign may not bring down an empire, but the Pozzo family cannot lay the foundation for Watford to secure its return to the Premier League.
Watford's frequent changes in management have become an inescapable fate.
On rough calculation, the next reshuffle will be the 12th in eight years, but the influx of fresh blood from a change of leadership is limited.
Captain Troy Deeney faces relegation with the team, although he could still participate in the UAE Super League.
Certain players may choose to leave, such as attacker Ismaïla Sarr who again expressed how unbelievable it is to play in League Two in September, but preserving strength is currently the top priority.
Arsenal coach Mikel Arteta said that the unpredictability of the match strength between the two teams makes the game more exciting.
Arteta also said that Watford’s forwards have been “cheating,” before switching to the footballing term “trying to stay up.”
Arsenal saying this is not without justification; Arsenal is ahead in the score, and Watford must press relentlessly to catch up.
Watford conceded a penalty kick right at the start.
In the first 40 seconds of the game, Craig Dawson accidentally tripped Alexandre Lacazette, and after a lengthy verification by VAR lasting approximately 3 minutes, a penalty was awarded. Pierre-Emerick Aubameyang scored the penalty kick.
Watford pressed hard, and Sarr quickly counter-attacked with the ball on the right side, with Roberto Pereyra and Troy Deeney providing cover as they closed in on the goal.
A former Labour Party member told The Daily Mail that even if Starmer expelled Corbyn from the party, they would still bring legal action.
What I mean is that he really brought the Labor Party back into competitiveness.
He has a long way to go in terms of policy, but he has already made an impressive start, having succeeded in re-engaging many who had previously abandoned the Labour Party and no longer considered themselves Labour voters, and viewing him as a serious and earnest person."
Blair indeed fears that the Labour Party is still seen as a party that only raises taxes.
He says, “Based on my experience, if you're a member of the Labour Party, you have to be very careful with taxation issues because the public isn't sitting there worrying whether you want to raise taxes; they're more likely to worry that you will, so you have to keep an eye on this mindset.”
The coronavirus will permanently alter the world economy (Opinion)
Just as various security measures became ubiquitous after the 9/11 events, virus screenings may become a part of our lives as well.
Necessary infrastructure investments to address future virus testing are imperative.
If coronavirus immunity only lasts for a short time, this investment can provide economic safeguards.
During the pandemic, many economies implemented policies similar to Germany’s “short-time work” (Kurzarbeit) subsidy.
According to this policy, both the working hours and wages of workers will be reduced, with the shortfall in wages subsidized by the government.
This move maintains the link between businesses and workers, laying a solid foundation for rapid economic recovery.
Improving the implementation of these policies is also important, which need to become a permanent part of economic recovery tools.
Remote work may become increasingly common.
There is evidence that working from home can be just as efficient as working in an office.
However, many companies are still reluctant to accept the remote working arrangement.
Since remote working has been widely tried and proved effective, this approach may continue.
As e-commerce continues to grow, remote medical care, video conferencing, online teaching, and financial technology are increasingly adopted, and the pandemic has accelerated digital transformation.
Companies involved in cross-border supply chains are struggling with various shortages and bottlenecks.
We may see many such companies shifting production back to their home countries.
Finance Minister Fiona Hyslop has said that the pandemic has had a "catastrophic impact" on all activities.
The measures taken to contain the spread of the coronavirus have affected Scottish festive activities and sporting events such as Highland Games.
Ms. Hyslop said, “While we have made a positive step, such as the resumption of drive-in theaters, most industries will not be able to operate for some time.”
We have engaged in close and constructive collaboration with industry leaders, who have proposed creative solutions for how to operate safely given their respective challenges.
In addition to direct funding, some of the funds will be used to promote innovation and sustain long-term recovery of the event activities.”
The Event Industry Advisory Group notes that the industry was among the first to be restricted by lockdowns and will likely also be one of the last to see full easing of restrictions.
Chairman of the group, Peter Duthie, who also serves as the CEO of the Glasgow Trading Commission, states that such sporting events bring nearly 6 billion pounds to Scotland annually.
He said, “It is necessary for the government to allocate additional funds to the industry, which has received widespread support from event organizers and numerous related supply chain enterprises that have suffered severe economic losses during prolonged lockdowns.”
Shivraj Singh Chouhan Confirmed Infected with COVID-19
Central India Chief Minister Shivraj Singh Chouhan confirmed Saturday that his coronavirus test results were positive. In a tweet he posted on Sunday morning, he said he was "okay," and praised the frontline workers fighting the pandemic.
The chief minister reiterated his warning issued yesterday, urging everyone to follow防疫guidance, such as maintaining social distance and wearing masks.
"Friends, I am fine. The frontline fighters risk their lives for others and deserve to be praised. I pay my respects to all the frontline fighters across the country," Chowhan wrote on Twitter from Chirayu Hospital in Bhopal, where he was admitted yesterday.
“Keeping a distance of two meters, washing hands frequently, and wearing masks – these are the most effective weapons we have against the coronavirus,” he said, “I hope everyone can use these weapons to protect themselves and their families.”
Zhouhan concluded with, “Be careful, everyone. I hope you are all safe and healthy. This is also what I pray to God for.”
On Saturday, Zhouhan said on Twitter that his viral test came back positive, despite having made every effort to avoid this situation, and warned, "A little carelessness can lead to contracting the coronavirus."
This morning, Chouhan said on Twitter that although the threat of infection remains, there is "no need to be afraid," and called for people with symptoms to get tested.
He says, "If you get infected with the virus, there's no need to be afraid. Do not hide your symptoms; inform the doctor immediately and begin treatment. Early treatment can lead to recovery."
Central Bank has over 27,000 confirmed cases, with 7,600 active cases, and 799 deaths related to the coronavirus.
Over the past few weeks, India has seen a surge in confirmed cases, with daily新增cases reaching up to 45,000 since July 23.
The total number of confirmed cases of COVID-19 is rapidly approaching 1.4 million, with over 467,000 active cases and over 32,000 deaths linked to the virus.
And what could be more suitable than a rock from Mars for studying rocks on Mars? says Professor Caroline Smith, curator of the Meteorite Gallery at the Natural History Museum.
Smith is also a member of the science team for the 2020 Mars mission, and he adds that scientists believe the rocks they bring back came from Mars.
The tiny bubbles within the meteorite are identical to the composition of Mars' atmosphere, which is why we are certain that this rock came from there.
It is believed that this Martian meteorite was formed when a small asteroid or comet collided with Mars about 60 to 70 million years ago, scattering debris in space.
One rock swept through the solar system and eventually landed on Earth.
This meteorite, now known as SAU 008, was discovered in Oman in 1999 and has been kept by the Natural History Museum since then.
On the instruments aboard the "Perseverance" rover, there is a high-precision laser instrument named SherLOC, which will be used to decipher the chemical composition of Martian rocks and determine whether they contain organic matter that could indicate the presence or continued existence of life on Mars.
Adding one SAU 008 is to ensure maximum precision for this operation.
Smith added, “We selected this rock particularly because its chemical composition matches and it is an extremely hard rock.”
"One of the Martian meteorites we found is very brittle, but this one is as hard as an old boot."
When Ingenuity finds rocks of research value, it will store them on the surface of Mars.
Then, these rocks will be recovered by subsequent robots sent to Mars and launched back to Earth for research and analysis.
A man was shot near Mambouin, nearby Virebi.
Last night, a man in western Melbourne was shot and urgently taken to the hospital for treatment.
The police arrived at Mambourin, near Vereby, around 8 PM after receiving an alarm. On Yard Street, they found a 20-year-old man with multiple gunshot wounds to his lower body.
The man was sent to the hospital for treatment and is without life-threatening danger.
The police said they are investigating this case.
Oliver Spencer's Sunday: "I don't dress up on Sundays; I wear loungewear."
How does your Sunday start?
I stumbled down the stairs and tripped over a dog while going to make tea for my wife.
After getting off the ferry, we drove onto A3 Highway and headed back to London to prepare for the start of the workday. Upon arriving home, the children went to bed, and after I walked my dog, I had another bowl of cereal.
I would be happy to return from White Island to London, as I know that I could not have a home there if I did not do so.
Kenyan police in Garissa arrested for shooting incident
Two Kenyan police officers have been arrested for allegedly causing two deaths in a shooting incident.
The shooting occurred on Saturday at the Soko Ng’ombe market in eastern Garissa County, where protests were ongoing over an alleged murder case.
Two victims are Aden Abdi Madobe and Muhiyadin Adow Shibin.
The Kenyan Police Service ombudsman has launched an investigation into the shooting incident.
The investigation process includes forensic analysis of the involved firearms.
Aden Duale, a Garissa lawmaker, called the incident "heinous" and condemned "police brutality" in a post on Facebook.
Several activists have also expressed concerns about police enforcement actions during the pandemic.
According to data from Amnesty International, since March, Kenyan police have caused 21 deaths, all due to failure to comply with curfew orders and wearing masks as health requirements.
At the beginning of this month, police in the capital city of Nairobi released tear gas and arrested several protesters participating in the annual Saba Saba march – which stems from protests 30 years ago opposing former President Daniel arap Moi's dictatorial rule.
A reporter in front notes that the protests this year have become more radical due to the anger sparked by police crackdowns during the pandemic lockdowns.
Enhanced security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China
On Sunday, security at the U.S. Consulate General in Chengdu, China was upgraded, and U.S. personnel were preparing to evacuate.
Three moving trucks entered the hall, uniformed police and plainclothes officers lined the streets on both sides, with metal barriers set up along the roadside.
China's Global Times editor posted a tweet stating that the US Consulate General in Chengdu was required to close within 72 hours, i.e., at 10 am on Monday, July 27th (0200 GMT).
China accuses some personnel of the US Consulate General in Chengdu of engaging in activities inconsistent with their status.
The spokesperson for the Chinese Foreign Ministry, Wang Wenbin, did not provide any details and merely stated that such activities interfere with China's internal affairs and harm China's security interests.
China demanded on Friday that the U.S. consulate in Chengdu be closed, as a countermeasure to the U.S. demand to close China's consulate in Houston, Texas.
Washington demands that China close the Consulate General in Houston “in order to protect American intellectual property and the personal information of Americans.”
Following the closure of the Chinese Consulate-General in Houston on Friday, a group of individuals resembling American officials broke into the facility through a back door.
Wang Wenbin said on Saturday that this action seriously violates international law and bilateral agreements, and China will make a response, but did not specify the measures.
Fiercely closing embassies further exacerbates tensions between the two countries over trade and industrial espionage, as well as human rights issues.
Vandals supply vandalizing weapons, and baboons in the zoo swing various tools.
The Northside Wildlife Sanctuary in Mercer County is notorious for its primates, which attack cars parked inside the fence, breaking windshields or rearview mirrors.
Some saw baboons at the North Wales Wildlife Park in Merseyside wielding knives, screwdrivers, even chainsaws.
Staff is worried that pranksters might arm these annoying primates, which could then pounce on stopped cars, ripping off wipers or mirrors.
Now, staff are concerned that visitors are giving them lethal weapons as "a joke," which is increasing their aggression, and there have even been reports of these baboons rifling through toolboxes.
A staff member of a园区 told The Sunday Times, "We are not sure whether tourists who wanted to see them attack cars provided them with weapons, or whether they stole them from pickup trucks and cargo vans themselves."
"They do indeed rummage through others' toolboxes and carry them around. One has seen baboons dragging a chain saw."
Another staff member said, “We have seen baboons carrying small knives and screwdrivers. I was wondering if tourists were giving them to the baboons.”
Chimpanzees attacks were visible, even to the repairmen in northwest England who knew about this zoo.
A repairman from Manchester Salford said that people knew what was going on at the zoo, and two of his clients were bitten by monkeys this year.
And they say that in one minute children wanted to see the monkeys riding in cars, and in the next minute the license plates were gone, but such issues brought good business to the repair shop.
Northside Zoo insists that it is just as safe as a McDonald’s drive-thru, as long as visitors stay in their vehicles during a 5-mile wildlife tour of the park. The zoo reopened on June 15th, taking safety measures to address the pandemic.
This zoo covers about 222.7 hectares and houses animals such as lions, tigers, rhinos, zebras, and camels, dismissing reports of them having weapons for baboons.
A spokesperson said, “We believe that many such stories have been exaggerated through hearsay and embellished, leading to situations where items being found in the park seem unbelievable.”
In 2012, Hyundai, the car manufacturer, placed its new-generation i30 car in the zoo with 40 monkeys for 10 hours to prove the vehicle's strength sufficient to ensure the safety of families with children.
Manchester United and Chelsea entered the UEFA Champions League, while Brighton and Hove Albion and Watford dropped out of the English Premier League.
On Sunday, Manchester United and Chelsea advanced to the UEFA Champions League by defeating Leicester City in the final day of the Premier League, while Brighton and Hove Albion and Watford were relegated, ending their five-year peak battle.
After Bruno Fernandes and Jesse Lingard scored, Manchester United defeated Leicester City 2-0, securing third place for Ole Gunnar Solskjaer’s team.
Chelsea defeated Wolves 2-0 with goals and assists from Mason Mount, securing fourth place and earning the final spot in the UEFA Champions League by passing Leicester City (which had been consistently in the top four during the season).
Leicester City will participate in the European league, and so will Tottenham, which drew 1-1 with Wolves in Crystal Palace and edged out Wolves due to a better goal difference.
Avoiding a play-off by securing the final two relegated spots came down to the final minute when Aston Villa scored in the 84th minute, only to be tied one minute later by West Ham United, 1–1.
Captain Jack Grealish scored a goal for Aston Villa, which may be his last match for his childhood club.
Bournemouth defeated Everton 3-1 but finished one point behind Aston Villa.
Watford were relegated as the second-from-last team after losing 3–2 to Arsenal.
Manchester United scored over 100 goals during the league season and won 5-0 against relegated Norwich, securing their place in the Champions League. This match was also David Silva's final Premier League game, which he had been playing for 10 years.
Kevin De Bruyne set a record this season with 20 assists and two goals.
Liverpool defeated Newcastle United 3-1, ending their title quest for this season, with the club finishing with a score of 99 points.
Additionally, Southampton defeated Sheffield United 3-1 this season with Danny Ings scoring 22 goals this season.
And Leicester City player Jamie Vardy scored the most goals and won the Golden Boot.
Gwyneth Paltrow hopes to instill good "values" in her children.
Gwyneth Paltrow aims to educate her children not just by “teaching them fish,” but rather by instilling good values in them.
Paltrow has two children with ex-husband Chris Martin, 16-year-old Apple and 14-year-old Moses, and she hopes they will uphold good values rather than relying on their parents’ fame.
"Why, among other things, I believe it is important to cultivate a child’s ethical character and ensure that their values align with either your own values or their own sense of identity," she told Rob Lowe in the podcast.
What actually happens is that I can show you the way, but if you just try like everyone else, you won't get anywhere.
She adds, “As parents, what I can do for my children is teach them right from wrong and what work is. I indeed tried to avoid merely teaching them fish.”
Paltrow also took steps to keep children’s lives as private as possible, ensuring that their social media accounts remained private.
Considering there were still new elections to be held, the Renewal Party ultimately agreed to join the coalition government.
Ted Cruz says Pelosi is “trying to solve problems with money and bring the U.S. government to a standstill.”
Washington –– Texas Republican Senator Ted Cruz criticized House Speaker Nancy Pelosi and the Democratic members of the House for their pandemic relief package, saying it aims to “solve problems with money and shut down America,” and negotiations over subsequent measures are ongoing.
Cruz said on Sunday during an interview with Face the Nation that the US is facing two simultaneous crises: a global pandemic that has taken more than 600,000 lives worldwide, and an "absolute economic disaster."
Cruz criticized Pelosi, who had been interviewed by "For the People," for “not addressing any of these issues.”
Cruz said that her goal is to solve problems with money and end the U.S. government shutdown.
"You especially need to look at the $3 trillion bill she is trying to push, which is nothing more than throwing money away rather than actually solving problems. Our goal should be to get Americans back to work and allow them to support their families."
Senate Republicans and the White House last week negotiated the terms of the fourth phase plan to address the pandemic, which is expected to total $1 trillion.
Last weekend, Chief of Staff Mark Meadows and Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin returned to Capitol Hill to further discuss with Senate staff. On Sunday, Mnuchin, in an interview with Fox News Channel, stated that Majority Leader Mitch McConnell of the Senate would unveil the details on Monday.
The most pressing issue facing lawmakers and the focus of the debate between Republicans and Democrats is that extended unemployment benefits have expired and will be canceled this weekend.
The Democrats want the “Phase IV” coronavirus relief package to continue including the additional $600 in benefits, while Republicans call for cutting this amount, arguing that some Americans receive unemployment income that exceeds their wages.
Cruz says that currently half or more Americans receiving increased unemployment benefits earn more than they did when working. He notes that some small business owners have encountered difficulties hiring due to this, as he learned from speaking with small business owners in Texas.
He says, “Of course they won’t come back to work, because the federal government is paying them sometimes twice as much as their wages.”
Cruz believes that Congress should focus on passing a recovery bill that lowers taxes for small businesses and removes regulations in order to get Americans back to work.
He said, “The Recovery Act is not about spending tens of trillions of dollars, but pausing payroll taxes, which amounts to giving a raise to every working American. This is the way that truly gets people back to work.”
Trump asked for the next rescue package to include a payroll tax cut last week, but Mnuchin said the proposal would not go through the legislative process because the Republicans do not accept it.
President Trump confirms that the next bill does not include a reduction in the payroll tax, but attempts to blame it on the Democrats.
Last week, Cruz objected to the cost of the new proposal, stating on Sunday that he disagreed with the measure proposed by Senate Republicans.
However, he also criticized the $3 trillion bill passed by the Democratic-controlled House in May, accusing his opponents of deliberately crafting measures that would harm the president’s interests before the November election.
He says: “The Democratic Party’s sole goal is to defeat Donald Trump, and they naively believe that the best way to do so is to bring the entire country to a standstill and close every school in America.”
Prime Minister Narendra Modi mourns the Kargil heroes and says Pakistan is trying to stab India in the back.
Today is the 21st anniversary of the Kargil War, and Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi, while paying tribute to military martyrs, stated that despite India's efforts to maintain friendly relations, Pakistan continues to try to stab India in the back.
Prime Minister Modi said during his monthly radio program "Mann ki Baat" that "Pakistan caused this disaster; they devised cunning plans to grab Indian land and divert their domestic conflicts."
He said, “Pakistan is shameless for trying to occupy Indian territory and diverting public attention from its internal turmoil.”
At the time, India was working to repair its relationship with Pakistan.
But, as the essence of evil is to fight against everyone for no reason.
Such people will only do evil, even if the other party wants to do them a good deed."
He said, “That is why Pakistan chose to ignore India’s efforts to maintain the relationship and stabbed it in the back.”
He added, “Imagine the enemy occupying high hills while our troops are fighting below them. But our high morale and genuine strength helped us defeat the enemy on the hills.”
"Our courageous armed forces demonstrated India's great power in the Kargil War," Prime Minister Modi said, recalling what he had seen and experienced during his visit to the war zone.
India celebrates annual events to commemorate the Kargil victory, marking the day in 1999 when the Indian Army reclaimed all Indian outposts in the Ladakh region that were occupied by Pakistani forces 21 years ago.
On July 26, 1999, the Indian Army announced the successful conclusion of the "Vijay Operation."
The war that took place on snowy highlands in the Ladakh region lasted for nearly three months and resulted in over 500 Indian soldiers' deaths.
Earlier today, several high officials and military leaders also paid tribute to the martyrs at the “Unknown Warrior Memorial” (Amar Jawan Jyoti) at the National War Memorial, including Defence Minister Rajnath Singh, Deputy Defence Minister Shripad Naik, Chief of Staff Bipin Rawat, Chief of Army Staff M M Naravane, Chief of Air Staff R K S Bhadauria, and Admiral Karambir Singh of the Navy.
Relations between India and Pakistan have been tense for several decades following the Kargil War due to a series of terrorist attacks.
India insists that negotiations and terrorist attacks cannot occur simultaneously, and Pakistan needs to dismantle all terrorist camps operating on its territory.
Online lending institutions warn that small and medium-sized enterprise loan schemes may not achieve their goals.
Small enterprises are often overlooked by large banks, and thus they increasingly turn to online lending institutions to meet their funding needs.
However, Mr. Callaghan noted that peer-to-peer lenders do not have an advantage over large banks, as they cannot access the same cheap funding, and he also warned that the small and medium-sized enterprise guarantee scheme is unpopular.
He said, “I believe this proposal is unlikely to achieve its aim. People call us expecting very cheap funding from us, rather than a loan with half the amount provided by the government.”
So, after we called them, they were disappointed because someone had expressed a desire for a discount, but our financing costs remained unchanged.
Mr. Callaghan added that such a scheme would be “a piece of cake” if fintech enterprises were able to obtain cheaper financing and then transfer the funds to small businesses.
This is somewhat of a dilemma – large banks can obtain funds at lower costs, but they prefer to avoid risks, so they do not lend in the same way. They will not access data unless there is collateral, in which case they feel secure. Otherwise, they would be uncomfortable, ” he said. “Unless we can get cheaper funding, I don’t think the market has any illusions about the popularity of this solution.”
Moula in Melbourne also participated in the formulation of the plan, CEO Aris Allegos stated that the 50% guarantee yield suggested in the government scheme should be transferred to small businesses, but different lending institutions would handle it differently.
He said, "I can understand that others may not have experienced such a cost structure."
This may involve their funding structure, as well as whom they lend to and how they assess the risk of these categories.
Mr. Allegos would not disclose exactly how much loan Moula had disbursed under the original proposal, but stated that the revised proposal “significantly reflects the changes needed,” thereby providing greater flexibility for both parties involved.
However, he is very concerned about the “two-speed economy” that is running and its impact on small businesses.
He says: "For small businesses, the biggest challenge that Moola and loan providers face is the so-called cliff, and how to manage it."
Overall, while there has been some growth in demand, it has not yet returned to pre-pandemic levels.
The online lending institution Prospa, listed on the Australian Securities Exchange, also refrains from disclosing the amount of loans it has disbursed under the scheme, citing this as sensitive commercial information. However, CEO Greg Moshal noted that the government’s expansion of the scheme has been well received.
He said, “We support various enhancements to the solution that enable small businesses to access a range of financing products tailored to their needs, including flexible short-term cash flow products.”
Virginia Beach removes monument to the Confederacy
Virginia Beach (Virginia) — On Sunday, Virginia Beach City Council removed a 115-year-old Confederate Memorial from the public park. Two days prior, the city council had unanimously voted to remove it.
According to media reports, workers dismantled the 27-foot (8.2-meter) high monument on Saturday morning.
The monument was erected in front of the former Princess Anne County Court in 1905, where slave auctions also took place.
On Thursday evening, the city council unanimously decided to proceed with the related demolition work.
The city council heard from dozens of speakers who viewed the monument as a source of division for many and constantly bringing to mind painful pasts.
Given recent protests and destruction of other monuments, the monument is currently completely covered and surrounded by locked gates.
The monument will be temporarily retained.
The city now needs to wait for a response from historical organizations or other relevant entities willing to handle this monument.
Protests continue, with the police attempting to use new less-lethal tools.
In Portland and across the country, violent clashes between law enforcement and demonstrators continue, with debates raging about how to reduce the use of deadly force by police.
Some companies are stepping up efforts to provide new high-tech tools such as police-controlled handcuffs, to replace more dangerous weapons like pepper spray, rubber bullets, and flashbangs.
Amnesty International analyzed 500 videos of protests from May 26 to June 5 and found that there were 125 instances of excessive use of force in 40 states and the District of Columbia.
Brian Castner, an Arms Investigator from Amnesty International, said, "We saw the misuse of tear gas, pepper spray, pepper balls, and 40mm impact grenades, largely combined inappropriately with various less-lethal weapons."
Some still hold the view that choice is important for avoiding the use of guns.
Corey Pegues, a former deputy inspector of the New York Police Department, stated, “We should provide police officers with various personal protective equipment instead of lethal weapons like guns, as policing is the only profession in America where citizens grant the power to take a life.”
There is a new tool called the Bola Wrap, a handheld device that can launch a 8-foot Kevlar rope to ensnare suspects.
Other new equipment includes long-range acoustic devices (also known as acoustic cannons), directed-energy weapons that use lasers to heat human skin, new shells made of materials such as foam and chalk powder, irritants, and electric shock guns.
Just days after George Floyd was killed by police, protests turned violent, and the stock price of gun manufacturer Axon surged over 18%, hitting an all-time high.
It is预计 that the market for low-lethality weapons will reach $11.85 billion by 2023.
Watch the video to learn about the new high-tech non-lethal tools used by police, how to regulate their use, and what risks these tools may pose to future policing and communities.
The UN says that Afghanistan is hosting thousands of Pakistani insurgent militants.
Islamabad – A United Nations report states that over 6,000 Pakistani insurgents are hiding in Afghanistan, with the majority being illegal Pakistani Taliban members responsible for attacking military and civilian targets in Pakistan.
According to a report released this week, the organization known as Tehreek-e-Taliban (TTP) has links to the Islamic State’s (ISIS) branch in Afghanistan.
Some members of TTP even joined an organization affiliated with IS located in eastern Afghanistan.
On Sunday, the Afghan government did not respond to an Associated Press request for comment on related issues.
Reports indicate that ISIS-K (Islamic State in Khorasan Province) in Afghanistan has been severely hit by Afghan security forces and US and NATO forces, sometimes also facing attacks from the Taliban in Afghanistan.
The report was written by the Analysis and Sanctions Monitoring Group, which tracks terrorist organizations around the world.
The report estimates that there are about 2,200 members of the IS group in Afghanistan, despite its leadership being weakened, which still considers a Syrian citizen, Abu Said Mohammad al-Khorasani, one of its leaders.
The report also notes that monitoring teams reported that two senior ISIS commanders, Abu Qutaibah and Abu Hajar al-Iraqi, had arrived in Afghanistan from the Middle East.
The report notes that although ISIS-held territory is shrinking, they still have the capability to carry out attacks of some scale in various regions of the country, including Kabul.
The group also seeks to win over Taliban fighters who oppose signing an agreement with the United States, referring to the peace agreement that was signed between the US and the Taliban in February.
The aim of this agreement is to allow the United States to end its 19-year-long war in Afghanistan and to hope that the Taliban will guarantee that their territory will not be used by terrorist organizations.
The agreement is also expected to ensure that the Taliban will fully commit to fighting against IS.
The second part of the agreement – and possibly the most important part – calls for talks between Taliban representatives and political leaders in Kabul.
Later that Saturday, the U.S. State Department issued a statement saying that its peace envoy Zalmay Khalilzad was still busy in the region, trying to push stalled negotiations that have been repeatedly delayed due to disagreements over prisoner-release plans.
This US-backed agreement calls for the release of 5,000 Taliban prisoners by the Afghan government in return for the Taliban releasing 1,000 government and military personnel as an olive branch before talks.
Currently, the Afghan government refuses to release about 600 Taliban prisoners, calling them high-profile criminals, but is proposing to release the remaining prisoners.
The Taliban rejected the government's proposal.
"This is the closest opportunity in history for both sides to commence internal negotiations and a crucial step towards ending the four-decade-long Afghan war," the US State Department stated.
"While important progress has been made in exchanging prisoners, further efforts are needed to solve the problem."
Earlier this week, the Taliban’s political spokesman said that the Taliban plan to hold talks with Kabul’s political leaders after Eid al-Adha (an Islamic holiday) next month, provided that the prisoner release work is completed first.
The biggest concern for Pakistan is the militants within Afghanistan, particularly those linked to the TTP, Jamaat-ul-Ahrar, and Lashkar-e-Islam, or the Balochistan Liberation Army, which launched large-scale attacks in southern Sindh and southwestern Balochistan that month.
Multiple Pakistani soldiers were killed in clashes with militants in southwestern Balochistan.
TTP organized a terrorist attack in Pakistan in 2014 that resulted in the death of 140 members of the Pakistani Army College.
Most of them are students, some as young as 5 years old.
The report notes that “foreign terrorist elements based in Afghanistan pose a threat to the security of both countries, with an estimated number ranging from 6,000 to 6,500 people, most of whom are members of the TTP.”
HSBC, Britain's largest bank, denies setting up a trap for Chinese company Huawei in the US investigation – RT Business News
HSBC, headquartered in London, through social media stated that the bank did not fabricate evidence as reported by Chinese media to have led to the arrest of Huawei's chief financial officer in Canada.
A WeChat article that is no longer available states that banks claimed their actions did not prompt an investigation by the US into Huawei, the world’s largest supplier of telecommunications network equipment, as scrutiny of Huawei’s alleged violations of US sanctions against Iran had already begun well before the banks’ involvement, in late 2016.
HSBC stated on Saturday that it has no hostile intent towards Huawei and there is no "frame-up" of Huawei, and added that it will never distort any facts about its customers.
In response to the information requested by the U.S. Department of Justice (US DOJ), HSBC Group merely stated objective facts.
HSBC did not fabricate the evidence nor cover up the facts.”
The platform blocked this article, citing it as “in violation of rules,” and the content is no longer visible.
After multiple Chinese media outlets, including state-backed newspapers, accused HSBC of colluding with Washington to set up traps for Huawei, HSBC quickly issued this clarification.
In December 2018, Canadian police arrested Huawei's Chief Financial Officer Meng Wanzhou at the request of U.S. authorities, and for the next 18 months, the company remained a focal point of diplomatic news.
Ma Xiaohu was charged with fraud for allegedly using shell companies to violate sanctions against Iran and failing to disclose the relationship between Huawei and Iranian company Skycom to banks.
According to new evidence cited by The Global Times, HSBC had previously been fully aware of the relationship between the Chinese company and Skycom.
It appears that the bank chose to “collude” with the US government in order to avoid harsher penalties imposed in 2012, when HSBC had already been forced to admit to money laundering and violations of sanctions, including the ban on Iran.
At that time, the bank was fined approximately $1.9 billion and was required to take measures to prevent similar misconduct.
In an article on the matter, the newspaper wrote: “There is evidence that HSBC was aware of this relationship but deliberately sold its clients’ confidential business documents to the US side in order to let the US government overlook the British bank’s improper behavior and avoid the penalties it should have faced under the Deferred Prosecution Agreement of 2012.”
The Chinese government-controlled newspaper adds in an open warning that if such collusion is confirmed with the banks, it may impose “severe punishment” on them, in China (one of HSBC’s major markets).
In recent weeks, this well-known bank has been criticized multiple times.
Last month, HSBC was criticized for supporting the new national security law implemented in Hong Kong, where the bank originated and contributed more than half of its profits.
HSBC finds itself in an unenviable position: in the West, US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo calls it “bowing and scraping”; in the East, Chinese media hail its “late but welcome support”.
Angola: Pedalé Hospital transferred under the supervision of the Ministry of Health
Rwanda – Republic President João Lourenço has transferred the supervision of the Pedro Maria Tonha Pedalé Hospital under construction in Rwanda to the Ministry of Health this Friday.
The Office of the President of the Republic stated in a declaration that this move is aimed at implementing the government's construction principles, expanding the hospital network, and ensuring better medical and pharmaceutical services for the public.
The document also notes that "in the current exceptional circumstances, this measure allows the Angolan people to have a differentiated reference hospital with modern technology and precision instruments for diagnosing and treating complex diseases."
The article notes that President of Angola’s decision also takes into account the country’s population growth, which has fueled a “massive demand” for the most diverse range of high-quality healthcare services.
The document also notes that this demand is reasonable, as hospitals encompass complex disease treatments, teaching, and research related to ophthalmology and maternal and child health, areas for which strategies and management should fall under the purview of the Ministry of Health.
Algeria: President Tebboune mourns Mahmoud Ghamama – Algeria has lost a patriot and a steadfast warrior.
Algiers – President of the Republic Abdelmadjid Tebboune paid tribute to late National Assembly Member Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, who passed away on Friday, describing Algeria as having “lost a patriot and a warrior who devoted himself to the cause of the people and the nation.”
President Tebboune wrote in the condolence letter: "Today, Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama has left us. Algeria has lost a patriot, a fighter who dedicated himself to the nation's cause alongside the people, who did not waver from the moment he took up arms. His resolve was unwavering, and he fought side by side with valiant mujahideen brothers until he took his last breath on this day of profound sorrow, and we could only listen to the will of God."
"Late Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace, came from a special generation of believers who were patriotic fighters who sacrificed for their country."
"In 1960, they joined the Tahart training camp of Wilaya VI at the height of their lives, joined the ranks of the revolution and liberated Algeria from the claws of inferior colonialism, sacrificing their lives."
The late leader had a fighting spirit dedicated to building a united and prosperous Algeria, and his name is thus widely known.
The President added, “In the far south, Mahmoud Guemama is a respected mediator. Once the spark of discord is ignited, he steps in to help his compatriots reach reconciliation.”
These qualities and values won Mahmoud Guemama the respect and trust of the people of Tamanrasset, electing him as a representative in the National People's Assembly for multiple terms, a messenger and defender of the region's concerns and aspirations of the people, and the voice of all Algerian people.
No obituary will suffice to praise Mujahed Mahmoud Guemama, may he rest in peace.
"Today, he left us at the most needed moment for him in Algeria," the president said.
Finally, President Tebboune said, “I pray to the Almighty Allah that the deceased may be bathed in the light of your holy mercy, in the endless heaven, and greet his arrival alongside the shuhada and the pious.”
I pray that Allah gives his family and loved ones patience and comfort… Only you have the power.”
Travel Guide for Coronavirus Pandemic: Countries Implementing Entry Restrictions for Bulgarian Passengers
More countries are implementing travel restrictions. Starting this week, the Czech Republic hopes to conduct coronavirus tests on seasonal workers from Bulgaria. Denmark requires that only Bulgarian citizens who have registered their residence, hold employment contracts, or possess proof of business travel can enter the country.
Starting today, people who have visited Bulgaria and Romania in the past 14 days must undergo mandatory quarantine in Italy. Finland only accepts passengers with 'legitimate reasons' for entering.
Austria requires mandatory quarantine at home even if PCR test results are negative. Germany also implements the same policy for arrivals. For the next 14 days, entry is not permitted to southern Romania without sufficient reason. Greece has taken stricter measures on its land borders.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced that as of Tuesday, persons arriving in Greece from Bulgaria must present a negative PCR test result to Greek border authorities, with the test needing to be completed within 72 hours prior to entering Greece's southern neighboring country.
They also announced that travelers must hold a corresponding test certificate to enter the country. The certificate must be issued in English and include the three names and number on the passport or ID of the traveler. If the certificate does not meet the above requirements, authorities in neighboring southern countries will not accept it.
Passengers must undergo RT-PCR tests at the laboratory by collecting oral or nasal secretions. Accepted test results include those from national reference laboratories, national health laboratories, or private labs certified by relevant accreditation bodies in the country (not necessarily dedicated to COVID-19 testing).
Greeks, citizens residing in Greece, and travelers with substantial professional reasons are not covered by this measure.
The Greek Ministry of Foreign Affairs also announced that passengers arriving in Greece on Tuesday, July 28, and Wednesday, July 29, who did not get tested before departure will undergo testing by Greek authorities upon arrival.
The following requirements remain in effect: passengers traveling to Greece by air must fill out and generate a QR code for the electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) at least 24 hours in advance, and submit the form to Greek authorities upon arrival in Greece. For more information, see travel.gov.gr.
Currently, there have been no changes to requirements for land border crossings between Bulgaria and Greece. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs explained that "all passengers entering Greece via the land border, regardless of their nationality, need to fill out an electronic Passenger Locator Form (PLF) and generate a QR code at least 24 hours before arrival."
The closure of the Ilinden, Kapitan Petko Voyvoda, and Makaza checkpoints has increased traffic flow near the border of Petrich. Crossing is not an issue, and there are no queues of trucks. Everyone is waiting to go sea from Greece. Passengers need to wait about an hour and a half to cross.
Three checkpoints are open at the border between Bulgaria and Greece. Currently, the section between "Ilinden - Exohi" and Ivaylovgrad is closed.
Dominic Cummings visits the SAS base and is taken hostage.
The Prime Minister's Chief Adviser experienced the feeling of being held hostage when he visited the base last week.
For some harassed civil servants, the idea of holding Dominic Cummings hostage by pointing a gun at his head might have an appealing twist.
The hardline Prime Minister’s Chief Advisor experienced first-hand the taste of it when he visited SAS headquarters at Sterling Line last week.
The Sunday Mail reported that part of his itinerary on Wednesday included visiting an "indoors counter-terrorism training room," where elite soldiers hone their skills in dealing with terrorists.
He also toured the Hereford base under guidance, inspected the military equipment of the brigade, heard a briefing on recent operations, including efforts to clear Islamic State terrorists from Iraq.
The important visitors to the Wallace Line were especially welcomed – to experience the “Close Quarter Combat Room,” where SAS trains on this two-story building.
Princess Diana had visited here as well; in a training exercise in 1983, her hair had accidentally caught fire from the sparks of a thrown grenade.
It is known that Mr. Cummings arrived by helicopter, and the base is named after Colonel David Stirling, founder of the regiment.
He also visited the nearby Pontrilas training area, which houses other classified units.
This visit is one stop on his tour of highly classified British security facilities, which has garnered attention from politicians and military leaders.
When news of its plan leaked earlier this month, Conservative Backbencher and Chair of the House of Commons Defence Select Committee Tobias Ellwood said that Members of Parliament should be afforded the same opportunities as ministers and civil servants to question defence and security matters.
The situation of requiring a special advisor appointed by political figures to provide testimony is rare.
According to reports, SAS senior officials used the activities on Wednesday to highlight the importance of maintaining the numbers of the Royal Marines and the Parachute Regiment.
As previously reported, units that have been sending large numbers of personnel to special forces might be downsized or merged to save costs.
A defense source said: "SAS consists of 250 soldiers, half of whom were previously paratroopers. The size of the Special Boat Squadron (SBS) is smaller; without the support of the Royal Marines, it may be difficult to sustain."
"Cummings was told that these units must be fully retained."
Mr. Cummings had previously complained that "millions of pounds are continuing to go down the drain in military procurement," and recently attended a meeting at MI5 and MI6 headquarters, and plans to visit the SBS headquarters in Poole, Dorset, and the National Cryptological Laboratory in Broughton Down, Wiltshire.
The Ministry of Defense did not respond to a request for comment last night.
"Sunday Morning" Dayshow: Honoring Rodgers and Hart
2020 was the centenary of Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart's legendary partnership, one of Broadway's most colorful musical duo.
In tribute to these two songwriters, Broadway star Jelani Alladin (from Frozen and Hercules), Christiani Pitts (from Kong and Four Weddings and a Funeral), and dancer Richard Riaz Yoder (from Dear John and Shuffle Along) performed a modern take on “Lady Stray Horse,” based on Rodgers and Hart’s original work. This special recording was made for the audience of Sunday Morning.
The drummer Bryan Carter provides musical direction and arrangement for the performance, along with bassist Russell Hall.
California's Larson County wildfire is under control.
Two wildfires have broken out in Lassen County, located northeast of Sacramento County in California, with an area burned exceeding 12,141 hectares. Firefighters are still battling to contain the fires.
The California fire department reported that as of Saturday morning, the "Gold" wildfire in the southern part of the Arden region was only 40% contained, with significant portions of homes and ranches still under threat from the flames, which could have a major impact on agriculture, livestock, and the wood industry in the area.
The fire has lasted for four days, burning approximately 8,498 hectares of land and seven houses, and injuring two firefighters who were sent to hospital.
Firefighters worked overnight to break through and reinforce the fireline under limited conditions.
But according to the weather forecast, it was expected to be windy and low humidity on that day.
The "Hog" wildfire that erupted in western Susanville has destroyed two buildings and approximately 4,047 hectares of land.
The California Department of Forestry and Fire Protection stated that the fire had been brought under control after firefighters worked through the night to clear and reinforce the fire line.
As of Saturday morning, the wildfire had been raging for six days, with 45% of it brought under control.
Watch out for those seemingly charming gardening schemes.
It may be an embarrassing matter for botanists, but I am indeed very much addicted to gardening tricks.
On the positive side, I tend to think of myself as an open-minded, or at least somewhat irrational optimist, and many things in the plant world are not easily discernible through intuition.
But I do not shy away from admitting that I have fallen into this gardening trick countless times.
While I believe in the value of trying new things, I avoid some of the most common traps in gardening that I've encountered, as I do not enjoy being "in hell mode".
First, perhaps the biggest minefield – grafting tomatoes onto potatoes.
Transplanting tomatoes onto potatoes was hailed as a revolutionary breakthrough, though in fact it is an old trick that was attempted back in the 1920s.
Tomatoes and potatoes belong to the same family, and their “pipes” inside are fairly similar, indeed creating wonders through grafting.
But the problem is that creating resource-intensive structures such as fruit and root crops requires a lot of energy for metabolism, and grafted plants need to meet the energy demands of two crops simultaneously, leaving limited energy to be diverted.
This means that the yield of these grafted plants is very low, quite different from what you see in the promotional pictures.
Moreover, the time of crop maturity varies, so harvesting potatoes requires killing tomatoes that have not yet matured.
Similarly, the promotional images also contain many misleading tricks.
I have seen roses that had been processed into sky blue using PS in some directories, and I've also noticed the number of fruits on certain trees as incredible.
Some merchants even “borrow” photos from my Instagram account and falsely claim them to be their test bases on online shopping websites.
These unscrupulous merchants' actions make you have to look at these promotional images with a skeptical eye, especially those from the internet.
If it looks too good to be true, it probably is.
Talking about blue roses, blue is relatively rare in the plant kingdom, so if you see new varieties of shining holly bushes, orchids, or cacti under high concentrations of what appears to be "disinfectant," it's likely that they have been sprayed or dyed.
If suppliers explicitly state the situation, it's fine, but they rarely do so.
The blue butterfly orchid will revert to its original white, but cacti and holly ivy will quickly wilt after being coated with an anti-reflective dye.
Finally, I must expose the bonsai set that promises to grow miniature trees, and there are worse bonsai seeds than such sets.
Bonsai is not a plant.
This is a pruning technique reminiscent of ancient trees on distant mountains.
Bonsai are typically mature shrubs that have been pruned and then guided to form new growth directions with wires.
I am aware that I will continue to be captivated by these horticultural tricks, which is one of the pleasures of experimentation.
But by avoiding these issues, you may have an opportunity to purchase a physical item that matches what the seller is displaying.
Spanish quarantine measures: Do I need to be quarantined in Spain?
No, but if you return to Spain after 12:00 PM tonight, you must undergo home quarantine for 14 days.
British Justice Secretary Humza Yousaf said: “We decided earlier this week not to enforce quarantine measures for those returning from Spain because the data showed an improvement in virus transmission.”
But clearly, the latest data warrants our concern, and we therefore have to overturn the original decision.
We know it will be disappointing. But we have consistently made clear that we will closely monitor outbreaks in countries, and if the virus re-emerges in any country, we may ask to have it removed from the list of those not enforcing quarantine measures.”
The virus remains rampant and deadly. Our top priority is to contain the virus, prevent its spread, and protect public health.
The government has not yet announced regulations for British citizens traveling to Spain, but if you plan to travel to Spain, it is best to check the regulations for your area.
Spanish Deputy Director of Emergency Health, Maria Jose Sierra, stated:
"There has been a major outbreak here. This may be the second wave. We need to keep an eye on the situation over the next few weeks."
Imam-Ul-Haq of the Pakistan team continues to compete despite injuring himself during warm-up.
The Pakistan team and the England team will face each other in three test matches and three T20Is.
Pakistan cricket player Imam-ul-Haq was injured when hit in the left hand during a team warm-up match in a derby, and the team doctor announced that he is now fit to play.
The Pakistan Cricket Board (PCB) stated in a release, "The left-handed cricketer participated in a warm-up match before the game began on the second day."
On the first day of the tournament, when PCB White Team's Imam clashed with PCB Green Team's Naseem Shah, he got hit in the left hand.
On Friday night, Imam was injured when his left hand was hit by a ball thrown by Shah and had to withdraw from the game, but he participated in the morning warm-up match and will return to the field on the third day.
Over 30 employees of a bank in Tamil Nadu have been confirmed to have COVID-19.
Officials from the government departments and a state-owned bank in Tiruguraberi, Tamil Nadu, India, stated that at least 38 employees of a major branch of the bank located in Tiruguraberi tested positive for coronavirus.
Officials say they have suggested that customers who have visited the branch voluntarily participate in virus testing.
Previously, a senior employee of the branch had other complications and later died from COVID-19.
Officials stated that recently, they conducted a large-scale medical screening at the branch, which led to the discovery of these positive cases.
The branch leader said that they have already completed the disinfection, and the bank may resume operations tomorrow.
Company health officials are contacting customers who visited that bank.
Tamil Nadu is the second-most affected state in India in terms of confirmed cases, with Maharashtra having the highest number of infections. As of Saturday, Tamil Nadu had reported a total of 206,737 confirmed cases.
India's Prime Minister will attend the groundbreaking ceremony for a temple built on a disputed site.
New Delhi (AP) – According to a trustee overseeing temple construction, Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi plans to attend next month the groundbreaking ceremony for a Hindu temple that will be built on the disputed site of a 16th-century mosque that was demolished in 1992 by Hindu militants.
The ceremony is scheduled for August 5th, when organizers describe it as a 'great day' for Hindus, though it marks one year since India's parliament revoked Jammu and Kashmir's semi-autonomous status, which until then had been the only Muslim-majority state in the country.
Modi’s supporters and opponents in India’s Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) will not miss this symbolic day, when the party has long promised to take back Kashmir’s autonomy and build a temple dedicated to the Hindu deity Ram on the site of a mosque that predates the Mughal era, with believers claiming it is the birthplace of Ram. The site is located in Ayodhya, Uttar Pradesh.
The coronavirus continues to spread across India, where it has reportedly become the country with the third-highest number of infections globally. In light of this, the World Hindu Council, an organization allied with the Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), has announced that the ceremony will be live-streamed on national television and that attendance and audience numbers will be limited.
The organization’s spokesperson, Vinod Bansal, stated at a press conference on Saturday that the temple will be “a timeless center for social harmony, national unity and integration, awakening Hinduism.”
Following the overwhelming victory of the Bharatiya Janata Party in the election, a century-long dispute over the site was resolved last year.
In November, the Supreme Court ruled in favor of the temple trust, ordering that five acres be allocated to Muslim petitioners elsewhere.
Hindu extremists have long claimed that Mughal Muslim invaders built a mosque on top of the existing temples in the ancient city of Ayodhya.
Violent communal incidents that followed rioting after the demolition of the mosque in December 1992 resulted in the deaths of approximately 2,000 people, of whom the majority were Muslims.
Meanwhile, the special court is still hearing the case of the mosque demolition.
An architect from Ahmedabad, Gujarat, India, hometown of Modi, proposes building a tower-shaped sandstone structure temple that is 161 feet (49 meters) high with five domes.
